SUMMARY:UC Carpentries: Core Elements of Citable and Discoverable Research
  Software
DESCRIPTION:Learn practical steps for making research software citable\, d
 iscoverable\, and reusable. Working in your browser with a GitHub reposito
 ry\, you will add an open-source license\, create a CITATION.cff file\, ma
 ke a versioned release with a DOI and improve project documentation and me
 tadata. This hands-on workshop is designed for researchers and the profess
 ionals who support them.\n\nThis event is co-presented by the UC OSPO Netw
 ork and UC Carpentries.\n\nThis workshop is free and participants can regi
 ster for individual topics or the entire series. View the complete schedul
 e and register.
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.library.ucla.edu/event/17386215
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ucla:LibCal-7056-17080330
SEQUENCE:0
DTSTAMP:20260904T160751Z
DTSTART:20261006T190000Z
DTEND:20261006T200000Z
SUMMARY:UCLA Open Source Meetup: Keeping the World on Time
LOCATION:Charles E. Young Research Library\, Data Science Center\, Room 11
 630L
DESCRIPTION:Join colleagues from UCLA and across the University of Califor
 nia for a hybrid lunch-hour conversation with UCLA Computer Science Teachi
 ng Professor Paul Eggert\, primary coordinator of the public-domain Time Z
 one Database (TZDB) and a longtime contributor to GNU software projects.\n
 \nTZDB does far more than keep the clocks on our phones and computers accu
 rate. It supports timestamps on documents and photographs\, preserves hist
 orical time-zone information used by software\, and must respond when gove
 rnments change their clocks with little warning.\n\nIn “Keeping the Worl
 d on Time: Inside the Time Zone Database\,” Eggert will introduce this w
 idely used but largely invisible infrastructure and discuss the technical\
 , legal\, political and collaborative challenges involved in sustaining it
 . The program will feature a 30–40-minute talk followed by an extended Q
 &A and conversation with the audience.\n\nIn-person attendance is limited 
 to 16 seats\, and advance registration is required. Bring your own lunch\;
  light refreshments and beverages will be provided. Online participation w
 ill also be available to members of the wider UC community.\n\nFeatured Sp
 eaker: Prof. Paul Eggert\n\nPaul Eggert is the primary coordinator of the 
 public-domain Time Zone Database (TZDB)\, which coordinates timestamps on 
 computing devices worldwide\, and a copy of which surely resides on your s
 martphone. He designed TZDB's naming standard and continues to manage its 
 data and code amid technical\, legal\, and political challenges. Over the 
 years he has also contributed to core free software projects\, including t
 he GNU C Compiler\, C Library\, Coreutils\, Diffutils\, Gnulib\, and Emacs
 \, and in 2022 he was given the Free Software Foundation Award for the Adv
 ancement of Free Software. After a career in the software industry that in
 cluded positions at the world's first independent computer software compan
 y\, the world's then-second largest computer company\, and startups\, he j
 oined the UCLA Computer Science Department\, where he is now a Teaching Pr
 ofessor.\n\nWho Should Attend?\n\nThis event is open to students\, faculty
 \, staff\, researchers\, librarians and technologists from UCLA and across
  the UC system. All experience levels are welcome\, from people curious ab
 out open source to active contributors and project maintainers.\n\nHosted 
 by the UCLA OSPO in partnership with the UC Open Source Program Office (OS
 PO) Network.\n\nLearn More About the Time Zone Database\n\nFor an accessib
 le introduction to Eggert and his work\, read the UCLA Samueli profile “
 Time Zone King: How One UCLA Computer Scientist Keeps Digital Clocks Ticki
 ng.” You can also visit the official IANA Time Zone Database website or 
 explore the TZDB source code and project documentation on GitHub.\n\nBring
  your lunch and join the conversation. Light refreshments and beverages wi
 ll be provided.
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.library.ucla.edu/event/17080330
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR